/**
 * Texas statutes, federal law, TREC rules and TREC guidance for Principles of Real Estate II —
 * the read register.
 *
 * THE RULE THIS FILE ENFORCES BY ITS SHAPE: HELD IS NOT READ. Every source below is a document on
 * disk whose hash is either taken verbatim from .prins2-drafts/HELD-SOURCE-HASHES.txt or imported
 * from the authoritative artefact that already carries it. Inside each, `sectionsRead` lists the
 * sections that were actually OPENED AND READ while the nine PRINS2-1 modules were authored, each
 * with a note stating the proposition taken from it. A chapter can sit on disk fully hashed and
 * contribute nothing; a unit is only as sourced as the sections read for it. Chapters that are held
 * for this project but that no read record names — Property Code chs. 11, 12, 13, 21, 41, 51, 53 and
 * 111, Estates Code chs. 101/111/112/201/251, Family Code ch. 3, Business and Commerce Code ch. 9,
 * Business Organizations Code ch. 200, Civil Practice and Remedies Code ch. 16, Tax Code chs. 11 and
 * 32, Government Code chs. 4001 and 4005, Local Government Code ch. 232, Insurance Code ch. 2501,
 * 26 U.S.C. §§ 1445 and 860A — are therefore ABSENT from this register entirely. They were not read
 * for this course, so they are not registered for it.
 *
 * WHERE THE ENTRIES COME FROM. Every entry below is transcribed from the nine per-module read
 * records in .prins2-drafts/read-record-T01.md … read-record-T09.md, written by the agents that
 * authored the modules. `reliedOnByUnits` is the union, across all nine records, of the units each
 * record names as relying on that file. `sectionsRead` lists only sections a record lists, and each
 * note states only a proposition a record states. Where a record enumerated several section numbers
 * against one run-on description, the note says so rather than splitting the description by guess.
 *
 * THIS REGISTER WAS RECONCILED ON 2026-08-29 AGAINST TWO DEFECTS IN ITS OWN INPUTS.
 *
 *   Defect A — the source manifest was incomplete. The first HELD-SOURCE-HASHES.txt was generated by
 *   a glob covering only texas/trec-sources/statutes/*.htm and texas/trec-sources/federal/*, so it
 *   never looked in texas/trec-sources/trec-rules/ or texas/trec-sources/articles/. On that partial
 *   manifest this register concluded that 22 TAC §§ 535.55/.62/.63/.64/.65 and the TREC
 *   buyer-representation article were "read without a manifest hash" and excluded them. Those files
 *   exist and are hashed. The manifest has since been regenerated over the whole tree (88 entries)
 *   and every hash this register carries reconciles against disk — see sourceValidation().
 *
 *   Defect B — sources fetched after this register's inputs were written. Modules 02, 05 and 08 were
 *   revised to teach sources their read records had originally recorded as unheld, and those records
 *   were rewritten by the revising agents. This register now reflects the CURRENT read records:
 *   Tex. Const. art. XVI § 50 and Regulation Z §§ 1026.2/.32/.33 (module 02); 42 U.S.C. §§ 4852d,
 *   9601, 4321 and 16 U.S.C. § 1531 (module 05); 42 U.S.C. §§ 3606, 3610, 3617, 12 U.S.C.
 *   §§ 2902-2906, 15 U.S.C. §§ 1691a and 1691c and Regulation B §§ 1002.2/.4/.5/.9 (module 08).
 *   The corresponding gap entries have been retired or narrowed to what is still missing, and
 *   Finance Code § 343.101 is now recorded as OPERATIVE, which is the correction the rewritten T02
 *   record makes expressly.
 *
 * SOURCE TIERS. Every registered source carries a `tier`:
 *   "controlling" — binding law or rule whose operative text supplies a proposition the course
 *     teaches as law, or on which the course's assessment or compliance logic depends. This covers
 *     every Texas chapter, the Texas Constitution file, every federal statute and regulation below
 *     except the two named next, and all five TREC rules. 22 TAC §§ 535.62 and 535.65 are
 *     controlling for the assessment design itself: the topic-quiz counts, the scenario count, the
 *     four examination versions, the 60-question versions, the 120-question bank, the no-true-false
 *     rule, the one-third overlap cap and the 70% unweighted passing score are all derived in
 *     ASSESSMENT_REQUIREMENTS from § 535.62(b)(7) and § 535.65. Occupations Code chs. 1101 and 1103
 *     are NOT substitutes for these administrative rules, and no unit that relies on a rule is shown
 *     here as covered by the statute alone.
 *   "guidance" — official explanatory material published by the regulator that is not itself binding.
 *     One source: TREC's 2026 buyer/tenant representation article, which supplies the open-house
 *     scenarios and the "subagency removed from TRELA but not eliminated as a legal concept"
 *     clarification. The statute alone does not supply either, which is why it is registered rather
 *     than treated as decoration.
 *   "contextual" — read and used for background, framing or naming, with no operative rule taken.
 *     Two sources: 42 U.S.C. § 4321 (NEPA declaration of purpose) and 16 U.S.C. § 1531 (ESA findings,
 *     purposes and policy). Each is a purpose section of a statute NONE of whose operative provisions
 *     are held, and module 05 states expressly that it takes no trigger, threshold, prohibition or
 *     permit rule from either. A findings or purpose section of a statute whose operative provisions
 *     ARE held (15 U.S.C. § 1601, 12 U.S.C. §§ 2601 and 2901, 42 U.S.C. § 3601) is controlling,
 *     because the module teaches the scheme it opens.
 *
 * RETRIEVAL DATES. Sources fetched in the 2026-08-29 authoring and revision sessions carry that date.
 * The Texas chapters carried over from the Principles I build (OC.1101, PR.5, FI.343, NR.21, WA.11,
 * BC.26) and the four RESPA files whose hashes match the Principles I register byte for byte
 * (12 U.S.C. §§ 2601, 2603, 2607 and the Regulation X excerpt) carry their recorded retrieval date of
 * 2026-08-27. No date here was guessed and no hash was recomputed by hand.
 *
 * WHAT THIS REGISTER CONNECTS RATHER THAN DUPLICATES. Two authoritative artefacts already carry
 * hashes for sources this register must show, and it imports them instead of restating them:
 * config/texas/trec-rules-source-lock.mjs (RULES — the five 22 TAC ch. 535 sections, each with its
 * own sha256, byte count, governing scope and provisions; and ASSESSMENT_REQUIREMENTS, from which
 * this course's assessment design is derived) and config/texas/regulatory-manifest.mjs (SOURCES —
 * including the buyer-representation article with its sha256). Neither of those files imports
 * anything at all, so importing them here creates no cycle; nothing else in the tree imports this
 * register.
 *
 * PROVING ITSELF. sourceValidation() recomputes every registered source's sha256 and byte count from
 * the file on disk with node:crypto and node:fs and reports mismatches, alongside the tier counts,
 * the gap counts by gapTier, and unit coverage. A register that cannot prove its own integrity is a
 * claim, not a record.
 */

